Checking the QEMU device models against drivers nobody here wrote
=================================================================

tools/qemu-patches has device models for parts this tree's kernels
drive.  Both halves of such a model are ours -- the model and the
Inferno driver that talks to it -- so a passing test can mean the model
implements the driver's mistake.  The README there calls that out and
sets qemu+patch as the weakest of its three evidence tiers.

These are the checks that break the circle: somebody else's driver,
against our model.  Zephyr is used as a tool and is not a dependency of
anything in this tree.  Nothing here is built by mk, and no Inferno
source refers to it.

Setting it up, measured on macOS/arm64 in September 2026:

	python3 -m venv zvenv
	./zvenv/bin/pip install west
	./zvenv/bin/west init -m \
		https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr --mr v4.1.0 zp
	cd zp && ../zvenv/bin/west update --narrow -o=--depth=1
	../zvenv/bin/pip install -r zephyr/scripts/requirements-base.txt

No Zephyr SDK is needed.  boards/arm/mps2/mps2_an385.yaml lists
gnuarmemb beside zephyr under toolchain, so an ordinary ARM GNU
toolchain builds this board:

	export ZEPHYR_TOOLCHAIN_VARIANT=gnuarmemb
	export GNUARMEMB_TOOLCHAIN_PATH=/opt/homebrew

west's own "west build -t run" would use whatever qemu it can find.  Do
not use it.  Build the ELF and run it by explicit path under the qemu
built from tools/qemu-patches, which is the whole point.


E1.  The CMSDK AHB GPIO, patch 0027
-----------------------------------

The register map in 0027 was written from the CMSDK documentation and
checked against Zephyr's include/zephyr/drivers/gpio/gpio_cmsdk_ahb.h,
which agrees offset for offset.  So Zephyr's own driver is the right
thing to make drive it.

tests/drivers/gpio/gpio_basic_api is a two-pin test: its README says a
board without two GPIOs wired together fails with "output pin not wired
to input pin?".  Patch 0030 straps gpio0 pin 2 to pin 3 behind
-machine gpio-loopback=on, which are the pins the test's own overlays
use.  gpio_basic_api/mps2_an385.overlay here names them; copy it to

	zp/zephyr/tests/drivers/gpio/gpio_basic_api/boards/

then

	cd zp
	west build -p -b mps2/an385 \
		zephyr/tests/drivers/gpio/gpio_basic_api
	<qemu>/qemu-system-arm -M mps2-an385,gpio-loopback=on \
		-nographic -kernel build/zephyr/zephyr.elf

Result, 19 September 2026, qemu v10.1.3 plus 0027 and 0030, Zephyr
v4.1.0, arm-none-eabi-gcc 14.3.1:

	SUITE PASS - 100.00% [gpio_port]:         pass = 1
	SUITE PASS - 100.00% [gpio_port_cb_mgmt]: pass = 3
	SUITE PASS - 100.00% [gpio_port_cb_vari]: pass = 1
	PROJECT EXECUTION SUCCESSFUL

Five tests, no failures.  The three callback suites are the ones worth
having: they exercise the edge and level interrupt logic, the polarity
registers and the per-pin masks, all written from documentation and
never before driven by anything but our own code.

Run it on a stock v10.1.3 as well, because the negative result is half
the evidence.  There it reaches the same point and stops:

	Check gpio@40010000 output 2 connected to gpio@40010000 input 3
	NOTE: cannot configure pin as disconnected; trying as input
	NOTE: pull-up not supported; trying as output high
	FATAL output pin not wired to input pin? (out high => in low)

which is what an unimplemented GPIO looks like from the other side, and
which mps2_an385.yaml has been claiming works under qemu all along --
it lists gpio under supported and qemu under simulation, and with the
block created by create_unimplemented_device() that cannot have been
true.  0027 is an upstream fix, not a local convenience.

The two skipped tests in after_flash_gpio_config_trigger are skipped on
this board for reasons of their own and were skipped before any of this.


E2.  The SX1276, patch 0028
---------------------------

Run, and it found two faults in the model and one in the driver beside
it.  Zephyr carries drivers/lora/loramac-node/sx127x.c, which is
Semtech's.

lora_send/ here has the overlay and the extra config.  Copy the overlay
to samples/drivers/lora/send/boards/ and

	cd zp
	west build -p -d build-lora -b mps2/an385 \
		zephyr/samples/drivers/lora/send \
		-- -DEXTRA_CONF_FILE=<path>/lora_send/prj.conf
	<qemu>/qemu-system-arm -M mps2-an385,radio=on \
		-nographic -d guest_errors \
		-kernel build-lora/zephyr/zephyr.elf

Result, 19 September 2026, with 0027-0033:

	<inf> sx127x: SX127x version 0x12 found
	<inf> lora_send: Data sent!

every second, and no guest-error output at all.  Their driver finds the
part over our PL022 and our GPIO chip select, resets it, configures the
modem and transmits.

One deviation, and it is not in the part under test.  Zephyr's
drivers/spi/spi_pl022.c declares a pclk in its config struct and never
initialises it from the devicetree, so without CONFIG_CLOCK_CONTROL it
is zero and every frequency is rejected with "Frequency is up to 0 in
controller mode."  Turning clock control on breaks this board's own UART
and GPIO, which reference &sysclk in a way that does not survive it
becoming a device.  So one line was added to their SPI instance macro,
.pclk = DT_INST_PROP_OR(idx, clock_frequency, 0), and clock-frequency
put in the overlay.  sx127x.c itself is untouched, which is what the
independence claim is about.

WHAT IT FOUND, which is the point of running it:

  - The model called being in FSK an error.  It is not: the part sits
    there out of reset, LongRangeMode only changes in sleep, and so
    every driver that wants LoRa passes through FSK sleep to get there.
    Semtech's did it five times in one boot.  Patch 0033.

  - The model applied LoRa's read-only registers on both pages.  0x1a is
    RegPktRssiValue in LoRa and RegAfcFei in FSK, which their init
    writes as part of a register table.  Patch 0033.  Neither fault was
    reachable by the Inferno driver this model was written beside,
    because that driver only ever uses the LoRa page -- which is exactly
    the circularity these checks are for.

  - Wiring the reset line at all exposed a third, in the GPIO model:
    it drove every pin whether output-enabled or not, and drove them all
    low at reset, so an active-low NRESET on an unconfigured pin was
    asserted from power-on and the part answered 0x00 to everything.
    Patch 0031.

  - And the NRESET timing check added for E2 immediately caught this
    tree's own driver holding reset for 58us where it had asked for
    1000, because os/inferno/armv7m/clock.c's microdelay counts
    instructions and not time.


E3.  Cross-implementation, partly done
--------------------------------------

Semtech's driver transmitting, an Inferno kernel receiving, the two
linked by -nic socket,listen=/connect=:

	ZEPHYR SENT: 24
	received 0
	notours 24

All 24 arrive.  All 24 are rejected, correctly: Semtech's driver sends
bare LoRa payloads, and os/inferno/port/sx127x.c speaks the
RadioHead-compatible four-byte header that lib/pycubed_rfm9x.py speaks,
so it filters on a destination byte the other end never sent.  That is
the right behaviour for a RadioHead network and it is what a PyCubed is.

The finding is the counter.  Before this, a filtered packet and a dead
link looked identical from /dev/radio0/stat -- received stayed 0 either
way.  notours and runt exist now, because that is not a distinction to
make an operator guess at from the ground.

What is still not done is the other direction with a peer that does
speak RadioHead, which is E3 proper: a host program on the same socket
running RadioHead's framing from RadioHead's source, and send_with_ack,
which this driver still does not implement.

Zephyr carries drivers/lora/loramac-node/sx127x.c, which is Semtech's.
Two things were checked before planning it, and both make it smaller
than it looked:

  - dts/bindings/lora/semtech,sx127x-base.yaml requires dio-gpios and
    describes "up to six pins", but the driver takes the count from the
    devicetree and guards everything past the first with
    "#if SX127X_DIO_GPIO_LEN(0) >= 2".  One DIO line is a legal
    binding, and the model already has DIO0.

  - reset-gpios is not guarded.  sx127x.c configures it and drives a
    reset sequence before anything else, and the model has no reset
    input.  That is the one thing 0028 must grow.

Then an overlay with an arm,pl022 at 0x40026000 -- Zephyr has
drivers/spi/spi_pl022.c, so that half is theirs too -- carrying an
sx127x child with cs-gpios on gpio0 pin 0, dio-gpios on gpio0 pin 1
where DIO0 already lands, and reset-gpios on whatever pin the board
patch wires to the new reset input.  Then samples/drivers/lora/send.

The outcome of E2 is a list, not a verdict: their driver is
LoRaWAN-oriented and will reach for registers, DIO mappings and modes
this model does not carry, and every such reach is a place where
os/inferno/port/sx127x.c has been agreeing with a model that agreed
with it.  See doc/radio-evidence.ms for what follows if that list
cannot be worked through honestly.
